('mathl1_001', 'math', 1, 'Algebra', 'Linear equations in one variable', null, null, 'If 4x − 9 = 27, what is the value of x?', '["4.5", "9", "18", "36"]'::jsonb, 1, null, 'Add 9 to both sides: 4x = 36. Divide by 4: x = 9.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_002', 'math', 1, 'Algebra', 'Linear equations in one variable', null, null, 'If 5(x + 2) = 45, what is the value of x?', '["5", "8.6", "7", "9"]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'Divide both sides by 5: x + 2 = 9, so x = 7.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_003', 'math', 1, 'Algebra', 'Linear equations in one variable', null, null, 'If x/3 + 4 = 10, what is the value of x?', null, null, '["18"]'::jsonb, 'Subtract 4: x/3 = 6. Multiply by 3: x = 18.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_004', 'math', 1, 'Algebra', 'Linear equations in one variable', null, null, 'If 7 − 2x = 1, what is the value of x?', '["4", "6", "−3", "3"]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'Subtract 7 from both sides: −2x = −6. Divide by −2: x = 3.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_005', 'math', 1, 'Algebra', 'Linear equations in one variable', null, null, 'What value of n satisfies 3n + 8 = n + 20?', '["4", "6", "7", "14"]'::jsonb, 1, null, 'Subtract n from both sides: 2n + 8 = 20. Subtract 8: 2n = 12, so n = 6.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_006', 'math', 1, 'Algebra', 'Linear functions', null, null, 'The function g is defined by g(x) = 5x + 3. What is the value of g(4)?', '["20", "12", "23", "27"]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'g(4) = 5(4) + 3 = 20 + 3 = 23.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_007', 'math', 1, 'Algebra', 'Linear functions', null, null, 'A line passes through the points (0, 2) and (4, 10). What is the slope of the line?', '["0.5", "8", "2", "4"]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'Slope = (10 − 2) ÷ (4 − 0) = 8 ÷ 4 = 2.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_008', 'math', 1, 'Algebra', 'Linear functions', null, null, 'What is the y-intercept of the graph of y = −3x + 7 in the xy-plane?', '["(−3, 0)", "(0, −3)", "(0, 7)", "(7, 0)"]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'In y = mx + b, the y-intercept is (0, b). Here b = 7, so the y-intercept is (0, 7).', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_009', 'math', 1, 'Algebra', 'Linear functions', null, null, 'A taxi charges a $4 pickup fee plus $2 for each mile. How much does a 9-mile ride cost, in dollars?', null, null, '["22"]'::jsonb, 'Cost = 4 + 2(9) = 4 + 18 = 22 dollars.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_010', 'math', 1, 'Algebra', 'Linear functions', null, null, 'A tank holds 60 gallons of water and drains at 3 gallons per minute. Which equation gives the number of gallons, G, left after m minutes?', '["G = 60 − 3m", "G = 63 − m", "G = 3m − 60", "G = 60m − 3"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'The tank starts at 60 gallons and loses 3 gallons each minute, so G = 60 − 3m.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_011', 'math', 1, 'Algebra', 'Systems of linear equations', null, null, 'x + y = 12
x − y = 2

What is the value of x?', '["5", "6", "10", "7"]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'Add the equations: 2x = 14, so x = 7.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_012', 'math', 1, 'Algebra', 'Systems of linear equations', null, null, 'y = 2x
x + y = 15

What is the value of y?', '["7.5", "30", "10", "5"]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'Substitute y = 2x: x + 2x = 15, so x = 5 and y = 2(5) = 10.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_013', 'math', 1, 'Algebra', 'Systems of linear equations', null, null, 'Pens cost $2 each and notebooks cost $3 each. Maya buys 7 items for $17. How many notebooks does she buy?', '["5", "2", "3", "4"]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'Let p + n = 7 and 2p + 3n = 17. Substituting p = 7 − n: 14 − 2n + 3n = 17, so n = 3.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_014', 'math', 1, 'Algebra', 'Linear inequalities', null, null, 'Which value of x is a solution to 2x + 5 > 17?', '["4", "6", "5", "7"]'::jsonb, 3, null, '2x + 5 > 17 means 2x > 12, so x > 6. Only 7 is greater than 6.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_015', 'math', 1, 'Algebra', 'Linear inequalities', null, null, 'A gym charges $30 to join plus $10 per month. Lena wants to spend at most $120. What is the greatest number of months she can pay for?', '["9", "8", "12", "10"]'::jsonb, 0, null, '30 + 10m ≤ 120, so 10m ≤ 90 and m ≤ 9.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_016', 'math', 1, 'Algebra', 'Linear inequalities', null, null, 'Which inequality describes all numbers x that are at least 4 more than 10?', '["x > 14", "x ≥ 6", "x ≥ 14", "x ≤ 14"]'::jsonb, 2, null, '"At least" means greater than or equal to. 4 more than 10 is 14, so x ≥ 14.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_017', 'math', 1, 'Advanced Math', 'Equivalent expressions', null, null, 'Which expression is equivalent to 3(2x − 5) + 4x?', '["6x − 15", "6x − 1", "10x − 15", "10x − 5"]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'Distribute: 6x − 15 + 4x = 10x − 15.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_018', 'math', 1, 'Advanced Math', 'Equivalent expressions', null, null, 'Which expression is equivalent to x^{2} · x^{3}?', '["x^{5}", "x^{6}", "x^{1}", "2x^{5}"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'When multiplying powers with the same base, add the exponents: 2 + 3 = 5.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_019', 'math', 1, 'Advanced Math', 'Equivalent expressions', null, null, 'Which expression is equivalent to (x + 3)(x + 2)?', '["x^{2} + 5x + 6", "2x + 5", "x^{2} + 6", "x^{2} + 6x + 5"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'Multiply each term: x·x + 2x + 3x + 6 = x^{2} + 5x + 6.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_020', 'math', 1, 'Advanced Math', 'Nonlinear equations', null, null, 'If x^{2} = 49 and x > 0, what is the value of x?', null, null, '["7"]'::jsonb, 'The positive number whose square is 49 is 7.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_021', 'math', 1, 'Advanced Math', 'Nonlinear equations', null, null, 'What are the solutions to (x − 4)(x + 1) = 0?', '["x = 4 only", "x = 4 and x = −1", "x = 3 and x = 5", "x = −4 and x = 1"]'::jsonb, 1, null, 'A product is 0 when a factor is 0: x − 4 = 0 gives x = 4, and x + 1 = 0 gives x = −1.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_022', 'math', 1, 'Advanced Math', 'Nonlinear functions', null, null, 'The function f is defined by f(x) = x^{2} + 1. What is the value of f(3)?', '["16", "10", "7", "9"]'::jsonb, 1, null, 'f(3) = 3^{2} + 1 = 9 + 1 = 10.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_023', 'math', 1, 'Advanced Math', 'Nonlinear functions', null, null, 'A population of bacteria doubles every hour. It starts at 50. How many bacteria are there after 3 hours?', '["300", "200", "400", "150"]'::jsonb, 2, null, '50 → 100 → 200 → 400 after 3 doublings.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_024', 'math', 1, 'Advanced Math', 'Nonlinear functions', null, null, 'The function h is defined by h(x) = 2x^{2}. What is h(−3)?', '["36", "18", "−12", "−18"]'::jsonb, 1, null, 'h(−3) = 2(−3)^{2} = 2(9) = 18.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_025', 'math', 1, 'Advanced Math', 'Equivalent expressions', null, null, 'Which expression is equivalent to 12x^{2} ÷ 4x, for x ≠ 0?', '["3x", "8x", "3x^{2}", "48x^{3}"]'::jsonb, 0, null, '12 ÷ 4 = 3 and x^{2} ÷ x = x, so the result is 3x.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_026', 'math', 1, 'Problem-Solving and Data Analysis', 'Ratios, rates, and proportions', null, null, 'A car travels 150 miles in 3 hours at a constant speed. How many miles does it travel in 5 hours?', '["200", "225", "300", "250"]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'The speed is 150 ÷ 3 = 50 miles per hour. In 5 hours: 50 × 5 = 250 miles.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_027', 'math', 1, 'Problem-Solving and Data Analysis', 'Ratios, rates, and proportions', null, null, 'A recipe uses 2 cups of flour for every 3 cups of milk. How many cups of flour are needed for 12 cups of milk?', '["9", "8", "6", "18"]'::jsonb, 1, null, '2/3 = f/12, so f = 12 × 2 ÷ 3 = 8.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_028', 'math', 1, 'Problem-Solving and Data Analysis', 'Ratios, rates, and proportions', null, null, 'There are 24 students in a class. The ratio of boys to girls is 1 : 2. How many girls are in the class?', null, null, '["16"]'::jsonb, 'There are 1 + 2 = 3 parts, so each part is 24 ÷ 3 = 8. Girls are 2 parts: 16.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_029', 'math', 1, 'Problem-Solving and Data Analysis', 'Ratios, rates, and proportions', null, null, 'How many inches are in 4.5 feet? (1 foot = 12 inches)', '["45", "54", "57", "48"]'::jsonb, 1, null, '4.5 × 12 = 54 inches.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_030', 'math', 1, 'Problem-Solving and Data Analysis', 'Percentages', null, null, 'What is 15% of 80?', '["65", "12", "15", "8"]'::jsonb, 1, null, '0.15 × 80 = 12.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_031', 'math', 1, 'Problem-Solving and Data Analysis', 'Percentages', null, null, 'A shirt that costs $40 is on sale for 25% off. What is the sale price, in dollars?', '["30", "15", "10", "35"]'::jsonb, 0, null, '25% of 40 is 10, so the sale price is 40 − 10 = 30.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_032', 'math', 1, 'Problem-Solving and Data Analysis', 'Percentages', null, null, '18 is what percent of 72?', '["25%", "40%", "54%", "18%"]'::jsonb, 0, null, '18 ÷ 72 = 0.25, which is 25%.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_033', 'math', 1, 'Problem-Solving and Data Analysis', 'Percentages', null, null, 'The price of a game increased from $50 to $60. By what percent did the price increase?', null, null, '["20", "20%"]'::jsonb, 'The increase is 10. 10 ÷ 50 = 0.20, which is 20%.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_034', 'math', 1, 'Problem-Solving and Data Analysis', 'One-variable data', null, null, 'What is the mean of 4, 8, 10, and 14?', '["8", "9", "10", "36"]'::jsonb, 1, null, 'The sum is 36. 36 ÷ 4 = 9.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_035', 'math', 1, 'Problem-Solving and Data Analysis', 'One-variable data', null, null, 'What is the range of the data set 12, 5, 19, 8, 15?', '["12", "19", "14", "7"]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'Range = greatest − least = 19 − 5 = 14.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_036', 'math', 1, 'Problem-Solving and Data Analysis', 'One-variable data', null, null, 'The number of books six students read are 3, 5, 5, 6, 8, 9. What is the mode?', '["9", "5.5", "6", "5"]'::jsonb, 3, null, '5 appears most often (twice), so the mode is 5.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_037', 'math', 1, 'Problem-Solving and Data Analysis', 'Two-variable data', null, '{"h": ["Tickets", "Cost ($)"], "r": [["1", "?"], ["2", "14"], ["3", "21"], ["4", "28"]]}'::jsonb, 'The table shows the cost of tickets.

What is the cost of 1 ticket, in dollars?', '["6", "14", "8", "7"]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'Each extra ticket adds the same amount: 21 − 14 = 7 and 14 − 7 = 7. One ticket costs $7.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_038', 'math', 1, 'Problem-Solving and Data Analysis', 'Probability and conditional probability', null, null, 'A bag has 3 red, 5 blue, and 2 green marbles. One marble is picked at random. What is the probability it is blue?', '["5/3", "3/10", "1/5", "1/2"]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'There are 10 marbles and 5 are blue: 5/10 = 1/2.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_039', 'math', 1, 'Problem-Solving and Data Analysis', 'Probability and conditional probability', null, null, 'A fair number cube has faces 1 through 6. What is the probability of rolling a number greater than 4?', '["1/3", "2/3", "1/2", "1/6"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'Numbers greater than 4 are 5 and 6: 2 out of 6 = 1/3.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_040', 'math', 1, 'Problem-Solving and Data Analysis', 'Inference from sample statistics', null, null, 'In a random sample of 50 students at a school, 20 said they walk to school. The school has 800 students. Based on the sample, about how many students walk to school?', '["400", "320", "500", "160"]'::jsonb, 1, null, '20/50 = 40% of the sample walks. 40% of 800 = 320.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_041', 'math', 1, 'Geometry and Trigonometry', 'Area and volume', null, null, 'A rectangle has a length of 9 cm and a width of 4 cm. What is its area, in square centimeters?', '["13", "26", "72", "36"]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'Area = length × width = 9 × 4 = 36.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_042', 'math', 1, 'Geometry and Trigonometry', 'Area and volume', null, null, 'A cube has edges 5 inches long. What is its volume, in cubic inches?', null, null, '["125"]'::jsonb, 'Volume = 5 × 5 × 5 = 125 cubic inches.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_043', 'math', 1, 'Geometry and Trigonometry', 'Area and volume', null, null, 'A triangle has a base of 10 meters and a height of 6 meters. What is its area, in square meters?', '["30", "16", "36", "60"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'Area = ½ × base × height = ½ × 10 × 6 = 30.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_044', 'math', 1, 'Geometry and Trigonometry', 'Lines, angles, and triangles', null, null, 'Two angles of a triangle measure 50° and 70°. What is the measure of the third angle?', '["70°", "60°", "120°", "50°"]'::jsonb, 1, null, 'The angles of a triangle add to 180°: 180 − 50 − 70 = 60.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_045', 'math', 1, 'Geometry and Trigonometry', 'Lines, angles, and triangles', null, null, 'Two angles form a straight line. One measures 115°. What is the other angle''s measure, in degrees?', '["245", "75", "25", "65"]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'Angles on a straight line add to 180°: 180 − 115 = 65.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_046', 'math', 1, 'Geometry and Trigonometry', 'Right triangles and trigonometry', null, null, 'A right triangle has legs of lengths 6 and 8. What is the length of the hypotenuse?', '["12", "7", "10", "14"]'::jsonb, 2, null, '6^{2} + 8^{2} = 36 + 64 = 100, and √100 = 10.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_047', 'math', 1, 'Geometry and Trigonometry', 'Circles', null, null, 'A circle has a radius of 5. What is its circumference?', '["10π", "100π", "5π", "25π"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'Circumference = 2πr = 2π(5) = 10π.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_048', 'math', 1, 'Geometry and Trigonometry', 'Circles', null, null, 'A circle has a diameter of 8. What is its area?', '["64π", "32π", "8π", "16π"]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'The radius is 4. Area = πr^{2} = π(4)^{2} = 16π.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl1_049', 'math', 1, 'Geometry and Trigonometry', 'Lines, angles, and triangles', null, null, 'An isosceles triangle has two sides of length 7 and a third side of length 4. What is its perimeter?', '["18", "28", "15", "11"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'Perimeter = 7 + 7 + 4 = 18.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_001', 'math', 2, 'Algebra', 'Linear equations in one variable', null, null, 'If 3(2x − 1) = 4x + 9, what is the value of x?', '["3", "5", "6", "4"]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'Distribute: 6x − 3 = 4x + 9. Subtract 4x: 2x − 3 = 9. Add 3: 2x = 12, so x = 6.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_002', 'math', 2, 'Algebra', 'Linear equations in one variable', null, null, 'If (2x + 6)/4 = 5, what is the value of x?', null, null, '["7"]'::jsonb, 'Multiply by 4: 2x + 6 = 20. Subtract 6: 2x = 14, so x = 7.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_003', 'math', 2, 'Algebra', 'Linear equations in one variable', null, null, 'If 0.4x + 1.2 = 3.6, what is the value of x?', '["0.6", "12", "6", "4.8"]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'Subtract 1.2: 0.4x = 2.4. Divide by 0.4: x = 6.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_004', 'math', 2, 'Algebra', 'Linear equations in one variable', null, null, 'For what value of k does the equation 2(x + 3) = 2x + k have infinitely many solutions?', '["3", "6", "5", "0"]'::jsonb, 1, null, '2(x + 3) = 2x + 6. The two sides are identical for every x only when k = 6.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_005', 'math', 2, 'Algebra', 'Linear equations in one variable', null, null, 'If 5x − 3 = 2x + 12, what is the value of 4x?', '["24", "15", "5", "20"]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'Subtract 2x and add 3: 3x = 15, so x = 5 and 4x = 20.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_006', 'math', 2, 'Algebra', 'Linear functions', null, null, 'Line ℓ passes through (−2, 5) and (4, −7). What is the slope of line ℓ?', '["−0.5", "−6", "−2", "2"]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'Slope = (−7 − 5) ÷ (4 − (−2)) = −12 ÷ 6 = −2.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_007', 'math', 2, 'Algebra', 'Linear functions', null, null, 'The graph of y = mx + 4 passes through the point (3, 13). What is the value of m?', null, null, '["3"]'::jsonb, '13 = 3m + 4, so 3m = 9 and m = 3.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_008', 'math', 2, 'Algebra', 'Linear functions', null, null, 'A plumber charges a flat fee plus an hourly rate. A 2-hour job costs $130 and a 5-hour job costs $250. What is the flat fee, in dollars?', '["50", "40", "60", "65"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'The hourly rate is (250 − 130) ÷ (5 − 2) = 40. Flat fee = 130 − 2(40) = 50.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_009', 'math', 2, 'Algebra', 'Linear functions', null, null, 'Which line is parallel to y = 3x − 2?', '["y = −3x − 2", "y = −(1/3)x + 5", "y = (1/3)x − 2", "y = 3x + 5"]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'Parallel lines have equal slopes. Only y = 3x + 5 also has slope 3.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_010', 'math', 2, 'Algebra', 'Linear functions', null, null, 'The equation C = 18 + 0.25m gives the cost C, in dollars, of renting a scooter for m minutes. What does 0.25 represent?', '["The total cost of the rental", "The number of minutes rented", "The fee charged before any minutes", "The cost per minute"]'::jsonb, 3, null, '0.25 is multiplied by m, the number of minutes, so it is the cost for each minute.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_011', 'math', 2, 'Algebra', 'Systems of linear equations', null, null, '2x + 3y = 13
x − y = 4

What is the value of x?', '["1", "4", "5", "3"]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'From x − y = 4, x = y + 4. Then 2(y + 4) + 3y = 13, so 5y = 5, y = 1 and x = 5.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_012', 'math', 2, 'Algebra', 'Systems of linear equations', null, null, '3x + 2y = 16
3x − 2y = 8

What is the value of y?', null, null, '["2"]'::jsonb, 'Subtract the second equation from the first: 4y = 8, so y = 2.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_013', 'math', 2, 'Algebra', 'Systems of linear equations', null, null, 'A theater sold 200 tickets. Adult tickets cost $12 and child tickets cost $7. Total sales were $1,900. How many adult tickets were sold?', '["120", "100", "80", "140"]'::jsonb, 1, null, 'a + c = 200 and 12a + 7c = 1,900. Substitute c = 200 − a: 12a + 1,400 − 7a = 1,900, so 5a = 500 and a = 100.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_014', 'math', 2, 'Algebra', 'Systems of linear equations', null, null, 'How many solutions does this system have?

y = 2x + 1
4x − 2y = 6', '["No solutions", "Exactly one", "Infinitely many", "Exactly two"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'The second equation is y = 2x − 3. Both lines have slope 2 but different y-intercepts, so they are parallel and never meet.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_015', 'math', 2, 'Algebra', 'Linear inequalities', null, null, 'Which value of x satisfies −3x + 4 < −11?', '["5", "−6", "3", "6"]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'Subtract 4: −3x < −15. Dividing by −3 flips the sign: x > 5. Only 6 works.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_016', 'math', 2, 'Algebra', 'Linear inequalities', null, null, 'A van can carry at most 1,500 pounds. The driver weighs 180 pounds and each box weighs 60 pounds. What is the greatest number of boxes the van can carry with the driver?', null, null, '["22"]'::jsonb, '180 + 60b ≤ 1,500, so 60b ≤ 1,320 and b ≤ 22.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_017', 'math', 2, 'Advanced Math', 'Equivalent expressions', null, null, 'Which expression is equivalent to (2x − 3)^{2}?', '["4x^{2} − 12x + 9", "4x^{2} + 9", "4x^{2} − 6x + 9", "4x^{2} − 9"]'::jsonb, 0, null, '(2x − 3)(2x − 3) = 4x^{2} − 6x − 6x + 9 = 4x^{2} − 12x + 9.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_018', 'math', 2, 'Advanced Math', 'Equivalent expressions', null, null, 'Which expression is a factored form of x^{2} − 9x + 20?', '["(x − 4)(x + 5)", "(x − 4)(x − 5)", "(x − 2)(x − 10)", "(x + 4)(x + 5)"]'::jsonb, 1, null, 'Find two numbers that multiply to 20 and add to −9: −4 and −5.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_019', 'math', 2, 'Advanced Math', 'Equivalent expressions', null, null, 'Which expression is equivalent to x^{2} − 36?', '["(x − 6)^{2}", "(x − 9)(x + 4)", "(x − 18)(x + 2)", "(x − 6)(x + 6)"]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'A difference of squares factors as a^{2} − b^{2} = (a − b)(a + b), with a = x and b = 6.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_020', 'math', 2, 'Advanced Math', 'Equivalent expressions', null, null, 'Which expression is equivalent to (3x^{2} + 5x − 2) − (x^{2} − 4x + 6)?', '["4x^{2} + x + 4", "2x^{2} + 9x + 4", "2x^{2} + 9x − 8", "2x^{2} + x + 4"]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'Subtract each term: 3x^{2} − x^{2} = 2x^{2}, 5x − (−4x) = 9x, −2 − 6 = −8.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_021', 'math', 2, 'Advanced Math', 'Nonlinear equations', null, null, 'What is the positive solution to x^{2} + 2x − 15 = 0?', null, null, '["3"]'::jsonb, 'Factor: (x + 5)(x − 3) = 0. The solutions are −5 and 3, and the positive one is 3.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_022', 'math', 2, 'Advanced Math', 'Nonlinear equations', null, null, 'If √(x + 7) = 5, what is the value of x?', '["−2", "32", "3", "18"]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'Square both sides: x + 7 = 25, so x = 18.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_023', 'math', 2, 'Advanced Math', 'Nonlinear equations', null, null, 'If 2^{x} = 32, what is the value of x?', '["16", "5", "6", "4"]'::jsonb, 1, null, '2 × 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 = 32, so x = 5.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_024', 'math', 2, 'Advanced Math', 'Nonlinear equations', null, null, 'What is the sum of the solutions to x^{2} − 7x + 10 = 0?', '["7", "10", "3", "−7"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'Factor: (x − 2)(x − 5) = 0, so x = 2 or x = 5. Their sum is 7.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_025', 'math', 2, 'Advanced Math', 'Nonlinear functions', null, null, 'The function f is defined by f(x) = x^{2} − 4x. What is the value of f(−2)?', '["−12", "−4", "12", "4"]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'f(−2) = (−2)^{2} − 4(−2) = 4 + 8 = 12.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_026', 'math', 2, 'Advanced Math', 'Nonlinear functions', null, null, 'The value of a car is modeled by V = 24,000(0.85)^{t}, where t is years after purchase. What does 0.85 tell you?', '["The car loses $0.85 each year", "The car was bought for $0.85", "The car loses 85% of its value each year", "The car keeps 85% of its value each year"]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'Multiplying by 0.85 each year leaves 85% of the previous value, which is a 15% decrease per year.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_027', 'math', 2, 'Advanced Math', 'Nonlinear functions', null, null, 'The graph of y = (x − 3)^{2} + 2 is a parabola. What is its vertex?', '["(3, 2)", "(2, 3)", "(3, −2)", "(−3, 2)"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'In y = (x − h)^{2} + k the vertex is (h, k), so the vertex is (3, 2).', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_028', 'math', 2, 'Advanced Math', 'Nonlinear functions', null, null, 'If g(x) = 3x − 1 and h(x) = x^{2}, what is g(h(2))?', '["5", "11", "25", "15"]'::jsonb, 1, null, 'h(2) = 4. Then g(4) = 3(4) − 1 = 11.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_029', 'math', 2, 'Advanced Math', 'Nonlinear functions', null, null, 'A ball''s height, in feet, is h(t) = −16t^{2} + 64t, where t is seconds after it is thrown. What is its height after 1 second?', null, null, '["48"]'::jsonb, 'h(1) = −16(1) + 64(1) = 48 feet.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_030', 'math', 2, 'Problem-Solving and Data Analysis', 'Ratios, rates, and proportions', null, null, 'A printer prints 45 pages in 3 minutes. At this rate, how many minutes will it take to print 255 pages?', '["18", "85", "15", "17"]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'The rate is 45 ÷ 3 = 15 pages per minute. 255 ÷ 15 = 17 minutes.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_031', 'math', 2, 'Problem-Solving and Data Analysis', 'Ratios, rates, and proportions', null, null, 'A map scale shows 2 centimeters = 15 kilometers. Two towns are 9 centimeters apart on the map. How far apart are they, in kilometers?', '["30", "67.5", "135", "60"]'::jsonb, 1, null, '9 ÷ 2 = 4.5 scale units. 4.5 × 15 = 67.5 km.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_032', 'math', 2, 'Problem-Solving and Data Analysis', 'Ratios, rates, and proportions', null, null, 'A runner''s speed is 6 meters per second. What is this speed in kilometers per hour?', '["3.6", "10", "21.6", "360"]'::jsonb, 2, null, '6 m/s × 3,600 s/h = 21,600 m/h = 21.6 km/h.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_033', 'math', 2, 'Problem-Solving and Data Analysis', 'Percentages', null, null, 'A jacket''s price was reduced by 20%, and then the sale price was reduced by another 10%. The original price was $150. What is the final price, in dollars?', null, null, '["108"]'::jsonb, '150 × 0.80 = 120. 120 × 0.90 = 108.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_034', 'math', 2, 'Problem-Solving and Data Analysis', 'Percentages', null, null, 'After a 15% raise, Dana earns $23 per hour. What did Dana earn per hour before the raise?', '["21", "20", "26.45", "19.55"]'::jsonb, 1, null, '1.15 × old = 23, so old = 23 ÷ 1.15 = 20.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_035', 'math', 2, 'Problem-Solving and Data Analysis', 'Percentages', null, null, 'A school had 640 students last year and 720 this year. By what percent did enrollment increase?', '["11.1%", "8%", "80%", "12.5%"]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'The increase is 80. 80 ÷ 640 = 0.125, which is 12.5%.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_036', 'math', 2, 'Problem-Solving and Data Analysis', 'One-variable data', null, null, 'The mean of five numbers is 12. Four of the numbers are 8, 10, 14, and 15. What is the fifth number?', '["11", "12", "47", "13"]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'The total must be 5 × 12 = 60. 60 − (8 + 10 + 14 + 15) = 60 − 47 = 13.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_037', 'math', 2, 'Problem-Solving and Data Analysis', 'One-variable data', null, null, 'Data set A is 2, 4, 6, 8, 10. Data set B is 2, 4, 6, 8, 40. Which statement is true?', '["The sets have the same mean and median", "A has a greater mean than B", "The sets have the same median, but B has a greater mean", "The sets have the same mean, but B has a greater median"]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'Both medians are 6. The mean of A is 6 and the mean of B is 12, because 40 pulls it up.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_038', 'math', 2, 'Problem-Solving and Data Analysis', 'One-variable data', null, null, 'The test scores in a class are 70, 75, 80, 85, and 90. If a score of 100 is added, what happens to the median?', '["It stays 80", "It increases from 80 to 82.5", "It decreases to 77.5", "It increases to 85"]'::jsonb, 1, null, 'With six scores (70, 75, 80, 85, 90, 100) the median is the mean of 80 and 85, which is 82.5.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_039', 'math', 2, 'Problem-Solving and Data Analysis', 'Two-variable data', null, '{"h": ["Week", "Height (cm)"], "r": [["1", "5"], ["2", "8"], ["3", "11"], ["4", "14"]]}'::jsonb, 'The table shows a plant''s height over several weeks. If the pattern continues, what will the height be in week 6, in centimeters?', '["24", "18", "20", "16"]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'The height increases by 3 cm each week: 5, 8, 11, 14, so week 6 is 14 + 3 + 3 = 20.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_040', 'math', 2, 'Problem-Solving and Data Analysis', 'Two-variable data', null, null, 'A line of best fit for data on study time and test score is y = 4.5x + 62, where x is hours studied. What does the line predict for a student who studies 6 hours?', '["89", "66.5", "99", "72.5"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'y = 4.5(6) + 62 = 27 + 62 = 89.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_041', 'math', 2, 'Problem-Solving and Data Analysis', 'Probability and conditional probability', null, '{"h": ["", "Soccer", "Basketball", "Total"], "r": [["Boys", "24", "36", "60"], ["Girls", "16", "44", "60"], ["Total", "40", "80", "120"]]}'::jsonb, 'In a survey, 120 students chose a favorite sport.

If one of these students is chosen at random, what is the probability the student is a girl, given that the student chose soccer?', '["2/5", "1/2", "1/3", "3/5"]'::jsonb, 0, null, '40 students chose soccer and 16 of them are girls: 16/40 = 2/5.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_042', 'math', 2, 'Problem-Solving and Data Analysis', 'Probability and conditional probability', null, null, 'A spinner has 8 equal sections numbered 1 to 8. What is the probability of spinning an even number or a number greater than 6?', '["3/4", "5/8", "1/2", "7/8"]'::jsonb, 1, null, 'The even numbers are 2, 4, 6, 8, and the numbers greater than 6 are 7 and 8. Together that is 2, 4, 6, 7, 8: 5 out of 8.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_043', 'math', 2, 'Problem-Solving and Data Analysis', 'Inference from sample statistics', null, null, 'A survey of 400 randomly chosen voters found that 52% support a plan, with a margin of error of 4%. Which conclusion is best supported?', '["Most voters definitely support the plan", "Between 48% and 56% of all voters probably support the plan", "The survey should have asked only supporters", "Exactly 52% of all voters support the plan"]'::jsonb, 1, null, 'A margin of error of 4% gives a likely range of 52% ± 4%, or 48% to 56%.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_044', 'math', 2, 'Problem-Solving and Data Analysis', 'Inference from sample statistics', null, null, 'A researcher wants to know how much time students at a large school spend on homework. Which sample would give the most reliable estimate?', '["50 students in an honors class", "50 students chosen at random from the school roster", "The first 50 students to arrive at school", "50 students who volunteer online"]'::jsonb, 1, null, 'A random sample from the whole school is most likely to represent all students.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_045', 'math', 2, 'Geometry and Trigonometry', 'Area and volume', null, null, 'A cylinder has a radius of 3 and a height of 10. What is its volume?', '["60π", "300π", "90π", "30π"]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'V = πr^{2}h = π(9)(10) = 90π.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_046', 'math', 2, 'Geometry and Trigonometry', 'Area and volume', null, null, 'A rectangle''s length is 3 times its width. Its perimeter is 48. What is its area?', null, null, '["108"]'::jsonb, 'If the width is w, then 2(3w + w) = 48, so 8w = 48 and w = 6. The length is 18, so the area is 108.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_047', 'math', 2, 'Geometry and Trigonometry', 'Lines, angles, and triangles', null, null, 'Two parallel lines are cut by a transversal. One of the eight angles formed measures 72°. Every angle that does not measure 72° has what measure?', '["118°", "18°", "108°", "72°"]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'Each angle is either 72° or its supplement. The supplement of 72° is 180 − 72 = 108°.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_048', 'math', 2, 'Geometry and Trigonometry', 'Lines, angles, and triangles', null, null, 'Triangles ABC and DEF are similar. AB = 4, BC = 6, and DE = 10. What is EF?', '["15", "24", "14", "12"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'The scale factor is 10 ÷ 4 = 2.5. EF = 6 × 2.5 = 15.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_049', 'math', 2, 'Geometry and Trigonometry', 'Right triangles and trigonometry', null, null, 'In right triangle ABC, angle C is 90°, AC = 5, and BC = 12. What is sin A?', '["12/13", "5/13", "5/12", "13/12"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'AB = √(25 + 144) = 13. sin A = opposite ÷ hypotenuse = BC ÷ AB = 12/13.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_050', 'math', 2, 'Geometry and Trigonometry', 'Right triangles and trigonometry', null, null, 'A 10-foot ladder leans against a wall. Its base is 6 feet from the wall. How high up the wall does the ladder reach, in feet?', '["8", "11.7", "4", "7"]'::jsonb, 0, null, '6^{2} + h^{2} = 10^{2}, so h^{2} = 64 and h = 8.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_051', 'math', 2, 'Geometry and Trigonometry', 'Circles', null, null, 'A circle in the xy-plane has equation (x − 2)^{2} + (y + 5)^{2} = 49. What is the radius of the circle?', '["7", "24.5", "5", "49"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'The equation has the form (x − h)^{2} + (y − k)^{2} = r^{2}, so r^{2} = 49 and r = 7.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('mathl2_052', 'math', 2, 'Geometry and Trigonometry', 'Circles', null, null, 'A central angle of 90° cuts off an arc on a circle with radius 8. What is the length of the arc?', '["2π", "16π", "8π", "4π"]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'The circumference is 16π. A 90° angle is 1/4 of the circle: 16π ÷ 4 = 4π.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_001', 'rw', 1, 'Craft and Structure', 'Words in Context', 'The museum keeps its oldest glass ornaments in padded cases. The ornaments are so ____ that even a small bump could crack them.', null, 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["ordinary", "sturdy", "heavy", "fragile"]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'Because the glass ornaments break easily, they are fragile, which means easily broken.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_002', 'rw', 1, 'Craft and Structure', 'Words in Context', 'Mateo was ____ to jump into the cold lake. He stood on the dock for ten minutes, testing the water with his toe and finding new reasons to wait.', null, 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["quick", "proud", "eager", "reluctant"]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'Mateo hesitated and kept finding reasons to wait, which shows he was reluctant, or unwilling.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_003', 'rw', 1, 'Craft and Structure', 'Words in Context', 'After weeks of warm sun and steady rain, the garden''s tomatoes were ____. The family had more than they could eat and gave baskets of them to their neighbors.', null, 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["hidden", "abundant", "scarce", "expensive"]'::jsonb, 1, null, 'The text says there were more tomatoes than the family could eat, so the harvest was abundant, meaning plentiful.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_004', 'rw', 1, 'Craft and Structure', 'Words in Context', 'While workers rebuild the old stone bridge, the town has set up a ____ wooden bridge. It will be removed as soon as the new bridge opens next spring.', null, 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["ancient", "invisible", "permanent", "temporary"]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'The bridge will be used only until the new one opens, so it is temporary.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_005', 'rw', 1, 'Craft and Structure', 'Words in Context', 'Chemists in the lab use scales that are extremely ____, measuring the mass of a sample to a thousandth of a gram.', null, 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["rough", "random", "careless", "precise"]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'Measuring to a thousandth of a gram is extremely exact, so the scales are precise.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_006', 'rw', 1, 'Craft and Structure', 'Words in Context', 'Buffalo grass is a ____ plant. Even after it is trampled by animals or dried out by drought, it quickly grows back.', null, 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["colorful", "resilient", "delicate", "silent"]'::jsonb, 1, null, 'Grass that springs back after being stepped on recovers quickly, which is what resilient means.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_007', 'rw', 1, 'Craft and Structure', 'Words in Context', 'Every October, the town''s pumpkin festival is expected to draw more than ten thousand visitors, many of whom travel from neighboring states.', null, 'As used in the text, what does the word "draw" most nearly mean?', '["sketch", "tie", "attract", "pull out"]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'The festival brings visitors to the town, so "draw" means attract.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_008', 'rw', 1, 'Craft and Structure', 'Words in Context', 'The mimic octopus can change both its color and its shape. Scientists think it uses this skill to ____ itself from predators by blending into the sand.', null, 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["measure", "conceal", "repair", "display"]'::jsonb, 1, null, 'The octopus changes color to blend in so predators can''t see it, which means it conceals itself.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_009', 'rw', 1, 'Craft and Structure', 'Text Structure and Purpose', 'Some animals use objects as tools. Sea otters, for example, float on their backs and crack open shellfish by hitting them against rocks they hold on their chests. Scientists continue to discover new examples of tool use in the wild.', null, 'Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the overall structure of the text?', '["It introduces a new topic unrelated to the first sentence", "It states the final conclusion of a scientific study", "It gives a specific example that supports the claim in the first sentence", "It disagrees with the claim in the first sentence"]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'The first sentence claims some animals use tools, and the second sentence gives an example: sea otters using rocks.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_010', 'rw', 1, 'Craft and Structure', 'Text Structure and Purpose', 'In 1945, engineer Percy Spencer was working near a machine that produced microwaves when he noticed that a candy bar in his pocket had melted. Curious, he placed popcorn kernels near the machine, and they popped. His observation led to the invention of the microwave oven.', null, 'Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?', '["To describe the life of a famous chef", "To explain how a common item was invented by accident", "To argue that people should stop using microwave ovens", "To compare two different kinds of ovens"]'::jsonb, 1, null, 'The text tells how Percy Spencer noticed a melted candy bar and how that led to the microwave oven.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_011', 'rw', 1, 'Craft and Structure', 'Text Structure and Purpose', 'Birds often fly into the large glass windows of city buildings because they see the reflected sky instead of the glass. To help, some cities now require new buildings to use glass printed with tiny dots. The pattern is barely noticeable to people, but birds see it and steer away.', null, 'Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?', '["It describes a problem and then explains a solution to it", "It lists events in the order they happened in history", "It compares two cities and chooses the better one", "It tells a story and then gives a moral"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'First the text explains that birds were hitting the windows (the problem); then it describes the special patterned glass (the solution).', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_012', 'rw', 1, 'Craft and Structure', 'Text Structure and Purpose', 'The Golden Gate Bridge rises about 220 feet above the water. Its builders needed to leave enough room for tall ships to pass beneath it into San Francisco Bay. Today, about 112,000 vehicles cross it each day.', null, 'Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text?', '["It explains why the bridge was built so high above the water", "It names the engineer who designed the bridge", "It describes what the bridge looks like at night", "It argues that the bridge should be torn down"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'The second sentence gives the reason for the bridge''s height: tall ships needed to pass under it.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_013', 'rw', 1, 'Craft and Structure', 'Cross-Text Connections', 'Text 1
Homework gives students a chance to practice what they learned in class. Students who regularly complete homework tend to remember lessons better.

Text 2
Some practice at home can help students learn. However, studies show that when students get several hours of homework each night, their stress rises and their sleep suffers.', null, 'Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?', '["By saying that students should get more homework every night", "By arguing that homework should be banned completely", "By agreeing that homework helps but warning that too much of it can be harmful", "By stating that homework has no effect on learning"]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'Text 2 accepts that homework has benefits but says large amounts can cause stress, so its author would agree only in part.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_014', 'rw', 1, 'Craft and Structure', 'Cross-Text Connections', 'Text 1
Bees pollinate many of the fruits and vegetables people eat, including apples, almonds, and cucumbers. Without bees, these crops would be much harder to grow.

Text 2
Bees help farmers by pollinating crops. Yet bee populations are shrinking in many places, partly because the wildflower meadows bees depend on are being replaced by roads and buildings.', null, 'Which choice best describes how Text 1 and Text 2 are related?', '["Text 2 argues that bees are not important, while Text 1 says they are", "Text 1 is about bees, while Text 2 is about butterflies", "Both texts discuss why bees are important, but Text 2 adds a threat they face", "Both texts describe how honey is made"]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'Both explain that bees pollinate crops; Text 2 also describes the danger of habitat loss.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_015', 'rw', 1, 'Information and Ideas', 'Central Ideas and Details', 'Camels are well suited to desert life. Their humps store fat that can be used for energy when food is scarce. Long eyelashes protect their eyes from blowing sand, and their wide, padded feet keep them from sinking into it.', null, 'Which choice best states the main idea of the text?', '["Camels can be trained to carry people", "Camels have several features that help them survive in the desert", "Deserts are becoming hotter every year", "Camels are the largest animals in the desert"]'::jsonb, 1, null, 'The text lists the camel''s hump, long eyelashes, and wide feet, all features that help in the desert.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_016', 'rw', 1, 'Information and Ideas', 'Central Ideas and Details', 'Main Street used to be one of the hottest places in town during the summer. In 2019, the town planted forty maple trees along the sidewalks. Now the trees shade the street, and measurements show it is several degrees cooler on summer afternoons.', null, 'According to the text, why did the town plant trees along Main Street?', '["To provide shade and lower summer temperatures", "To block noise from the highway", "To attract more birds to the town", "To replace trees damaged by a storm"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'The text says the trees were planted to shade the sidewalks and cool the street during hot summers.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_017', 'rw', 1, 'Information and Ideas', 'Central Ideas and Details', 'Many public libraries today offer far more than books. Visitors can borrow tools, telescopes, and even musical instruments. Libraries also host coding classes, job fairs, and free internet access for people who don''t have it at home.', null, 'Which choice best states the main idea of the text?', '["Libraries no longer lend books", "Libraries have changed to offer much more than books", "Libraries are closing because of the internet", "Most people prefer e-books to printed books"]'::jsonb, 1, null, 'The text explains that libraries now lend tools, offer classes, and provide internet access in addition to books.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_018', 'rw', 1, 'Information and Ideas', 'Central Ideas and Details', 'In the early 1920s, inventor Garrett Morgan witnessed a serious accident at a busy intersection. Determined to make roads safer, he designed a traffic signal that included a warning position, giving drivers time to stop before the direction of traffic changed.', null, 'Based on the text, what did Garrett Morgan do after he saw a car accident at an intersection?', '["He designed an improved traffic signal", "He stopped driving his own car", "He became a police officer", "He opened a driving school"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'The text says the accident led Morgan to invent a traffic signal with a warning position.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_019', 'rw', 1, 'Information and Ideas', 'Command of Evidence', 'Students at Lincoln Middle School voted for a new after-school sport. The results showed that ____.', '{"h": ["Sport", "Votes"], "r": [["Soccer", "42"], ["Basketball", "31"], ["Swimming", "18"], ["Tennis", "9"]]}'::jsonb, 'Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?', '["more students chose soccer than any other sport", "basketball was the least popular sport", "exactly half of the students chose swimming", "more students chose tennis than soccer"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'Soccer has 42 votes, more than basketball (31), swimming (18), or tennis (9).', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_020', 'rw', 1, 'Information and Ideas', 'Command of Evidence', 'This year, Hillside School made lunch period 10 minutes longer. A student reporter claims the change is helping students.', null, 'Which quotation from a student''s survey would best support the claim that the new lunch schedule is helping students?', '["\"The cafeteria should serve pizza more often.\"", "\"The gym is too small for everyone.\"", "\"Now I have time to finish my lunch and still talk with my friends.\"", "\"I usually bring my lunch from home.\""]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'Only this quotation describes a benefit of the new schedule.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_021', 'rw', 1, 'Information and Ideas', 'Command of Evidence', 'A scientist hypothesizes that playing calm music near bean plants helps them grow faster.', null, 'Which finding, if true, would most directly support the scientist''s hypothesis?', '["Some plants grow better in shade than in sun", "Music played louder in the afternoon than in the morning", "Plants grown in silence needed more water", "Plants given music grew taller than identical plants grown in silence"]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'The hypothesis is that music helps plants grow, so plants with music growing taller than plants without it supports it.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_022', 'rw', 1, 'Information and Ideas', 'Command of Evidence', 'A student claims that Town B is much wetter than Town A.', '{"h": ["Town", "Yearly rain (inches)"], "r": [["Town A", "12"], ["Town B", "48"], ["Town C", "30"]]}'::jsonb, 'Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the student''s claim?', '["Town C received 30 inches of rain", "Town A received 12 inches of rain", "Both towns received some rain during the year", "Town B received 48 inches of rain, while Town A received only 12 inches"]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'The claim compares Town B with Town A, so the best evidence gives both towns'' totals and shows B''s is much higher.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_023', 'rw', 1, 'Information and Ideas', 'Inferences', 'When Ana came home, the water bowl was tipped over, there was a puddle on the floor, and her dog was hiding under the bed with wet paws. Ana guessed that ____', null, 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', '["the vase had been broken for years", "the dog was afraid of water", "the family wanted to buy a new rug", "the dog had probably been the one to knock it over"]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'The dog is wet, there''s a puddle, and it''s hiding: clues that it knocked the water bowl over.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_024', 'rw', 1, 'Information and Ideas', 'Inferences', 'Paleontologists have found many fossils of fish and clams in a desert in Egypt. Because fish and clams can live only in water, researchers conclude that ____', null, 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', '["the fossils show that the area was once covered by water", "the desert has always been dry", "the fossils were brought there by tourists", "fish can live without water"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'Fish fossils in a desert suggest that water once covered the area.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_025', 'rw', 1, 'Information and Ideas', 'Inferences', 'Light travels very fast, but not instantly. Light from the Sun takes about eight minutes to reach Earth, and light from some faraway stars takes thousands of years. This means that when we look at those stars at night, ____', null, 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', '["light travels faster than anything can be measured", "all stars are the same distance from Earth", "the light from distant stars left them long ago", "stars do not give off light"]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'Light takes time to travel, so when we see distant stars we are seeing light that left them long ago.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_026', 'rw', 1, 'Information and Ideas', 'Inferences', 'For years, residents of the Eastside neighborhood had to walk two miles to the nearest bus stop. More than 600 people signed a petition asking for a new route. Since the city has now added a stop in the neighborhood, ____', null, 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', '["no one in the neighborhood owns a car", "most residents prefer to walk", "the new bus route will likely be used by many riders", "the bus route will be removed next year"]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'If many people asked for the route and there was no bus before, it will likely have many riders.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_027', 'rw', 1, 'Standard English Conventions', 'Boundaries', 'Jupiter is one of the brightest objects in the night ____ is easy to spot even from a city.', null, 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["sky; it", "sky, it", "sky. And it", "sky it"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'Two complete sentences can be joined with a semicolon. A comma alone would create a comma splice.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_028', 'rw', 1, 'Standard English Conventions', 'Boundaries', 'The Grand Canyon region stretches across parts of several ____ Utah, Nevada, and New Mexico.', null, 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["states: Arizona,", "states. Arizona,", "states, Arizona,", "states; Arizona,"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'A colon can introduce a list that follows a complete sentence.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_029', 'rw', 1, 'Standard English Conventions', 'Boundaries', 'Amelia ____ was the first woman to fly alone across the Atlantic Ocean, disappeared during a flight over the Pacific in 1937.', null, 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["Earhart. Who", "Earhart; who", "Earhart, who", "Earhart who"]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'The clause "who was the first woman…" adds extra information about Earhart, so it is set off with commas.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_030', 'rw', 1, 'Standard English Conventions', 'Boundaries', 'The orchestra had only two weeks to learn the new piece. The musicians stayed late after each ____ they would be ready for the concert.', null, 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["practice so,", "practice, so", "practice; so,", "practice so"]'::jsonb, 1, null, 'A comma comes before "so" when it joins two complete sentences.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_031', 'rw', 1, 'Standard English Conventions', 'Boundaries', 'Grandma Rosa''s family has raised sheep on the same ____ wool from the sheep is spun into yarn and sold at the county fair.', null, 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["farm the", "farm. The", "farm, the", "farm and, the"]'::jsonb, 1, null, 'These are two complete sentences, so a period separates them.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_032', 'rw', 1, 'Standard English Conventions', 'Boundaries', 'Many ____ around the world have built rooftop gardens to grow food and cool their buildings.', null, 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["cities,", "cities:", "cities", "cities;"]'::jsonb, 2, null, '"Many cities around the world" is the subject and "have built" is the verb. No punctuation should separate a subject from its verb.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_033', 'rw', 1, 'Standard English Conventions', 'Form, Structure, and Sense', 'The collection of rare coins in the city museum ____ worth more than a million dollars.', null, 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["were", "have been", "are", "is"]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'The subject is "collection" (singular), so the verb should be "is."', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_034', 'rw', 1, 'Standard English Conventions', 'Form, Structure, and Sense', 'After the science fair, the students packed up ____ posters and models and carried them back to class.', null, 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["its", "their", "his", "her"]'::jsonb, 1, null, 'The pronoun refers to "students" (plural), so "their" is correct.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_035', 'rw', 1, 'Standard English Conventions', 'Form, Structure, and Sense', 'In 1922, archaeologist Howard Carter ____ the tomb of the ancient Egyptian king Tutankhamun.', null, 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["discovers", "will discover", "is discovering", "discovered"]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'The sentence describes an event in 1922, so the past tense "discovered" is needed.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_036', 'rw', 1, 'Standard English Conventions', 'Form, Structure, and Sense', 'The art teacher hung all of the ____ paintings in the front hallway for parents to see.', null, 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["students", "students''", "student''s", "students''s"]'::jsonb, 1, null, 'The paintings belong to many students, so the plural possessive "students''" is correct.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_037', 'rw', 1, 'Standard English Conventions', 'Form, Structure, and Sense', 'Each of the hiking trails in the park ____ past at least one waterfall.', null, 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["running", "have run", "run", "runs"]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'The subject "Each of the trails" is singular (each), so the verb should be "runs."', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_038', 'rw', 1, 'Standard English Conventions', 'Form, Structure, and Sense', 'Astronomers first spotted the comet in March. By June, ____ was bright enough to see without a telescope.', null, 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["it", "they", "those", "them"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'The pronoun refers to "the comet," which is singular.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_039', 'rw', 1, 'Expression of Ideas', 'Transitions', 'Many people picture deserts as hot, sandy places. ____ Antarctica is also considered a desert because it receives very little precipitation.', null, 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["Similarly,", "In addition,", "For example,", "However,"]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'The second sentence contrasts with the first (most think deserts are hot, but some are cold), so "However" fits.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_040', 'rw', 1, 'Expression of Ideas', 'Transitions', 'Some animals travel enormous distances each year. ____ the Arctic tern flies from the Arctic to Antarctica and back, a round trip of about 44,000 miles.', null, 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["Instead,", "For example,", "Finally,", "However,"]'::jsonb, 1, null, 'The second sentence gives an example of an animal that migrates long distances.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_041', 'rw', 1, 'Expression of Ideas', 'Transitions', 'Priya practiced her speech every day for a month and asked her family for feedback. ____ she won first place at the school speech contest.', null, 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["For instance,", "As a result,", "Nevertheless,", "In contrast,"]'::jsonb, 1, null, 'Practice improved her skill, and the win is the result of that improvement.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_042', 'rw', 1, 'Expression of Ideas', 'Transitions', 'To make paper by hand, first soak scraps of old paper in water until they become pulp. ____ spread the pulp on a screen and let it dry.', null, 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["Then,", "In contrast,", "However,", "For example,"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'The text lists steps in order, so "Then" fits the next step.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_043', 'rw', 1, 'Expression of Ideas', 'Transitions', 'The arctic fox''s coat turns white in winter, helping it blend in with the snow. ____ the snowshoe hare grows white fur in winter to hide from predators.', null, 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["Similarly,", "As a result,", "However,", "Instead,"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'Both sentences describe animals that change color for protection, so "Similarly" fits.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_044', 'rw', 1, 'Expression of Ideas', 'Transitions', 'The city council decided not to build a new parking lot downtown. ____ it used the land to create a park with a playground and a community garden.', null, 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["In addition,", "Similarly,", "Instead,", "For example,"]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'The second sentence tells what the city did in place of building a new parking lot.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_045', 'rw', 1, 'Expression of Ideas', 'Rhetorical Synthesis', 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• Coast redwoods grow along the coast of Northern California.
• They are the tallest trees on Earth.
• Some are more than 350 feet tall.
• They can live for more than 2,000 years.
• They absorb water from coastal fog.', null, 'The student wants to emphasize how tall the redwood trees can grow. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '["Coast redwoods can live for more than 2,000 years.", "Fog helps coast redwoods get water.", "Coast redwoods can grow taller than 350 feet, making them the tallest trees on Earth.", "Coast redwoods grow along the coast of California."]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'Only this choice focuses on the trees'' height.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_046', 'rw', 1, 'Expression of Ideas', 'Rhetorical Synthesis', 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• Mary Jackson was born in Hampton, Virginia, in 1921.
• She studied math and physical science in college.
• In 1958, she became NASA''s first Black female engineer.
• Later, she worked to help women and minorities advance at NASA.', null, 'The student wants to explain what Mary Jackson is known for. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '["Mary Jackson was NASA''s first Black female engineer, and she later worked to help other women advance in their careers.", "Mary Jackson was born in Virginia in 1921.", "Mary Jackson studied math and physical science.", "Mary Jackson worked in Virginia."]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'This choice states her major achievements, which is what she is known for.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl1_047', 'rw', 1, 'Expression of Ideas', 'Rhetorical Synthesis', 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• Earth is about 7,900 miles in diameter.
• Mars is about 4,200 miles in diameter.
• Mars is often called the Red Planet.
• Earth has one moon; Mars has two.', null, 'The student wants to compare the sizes of the two planets. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '["Mars is often called the Red Planet.", "Earth has one moon, while Mars has two.", "Earth is about 7,900 miles across, nearly twice as wide as Mars at about 4,200 miles.", "Earth and Mars both orbit the Sun."]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'Only this choice compares the planets'' sizes using the numbers from the notes.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_001', 'rw', 2, 'Craft and Structure', 'Words in Context', 'When a team announced that it had grown tomatoes in soil similar to the Moon''s, other scientists were initially ____. They refused to accept the result until they could repeat the experiment in their own labs.', null, 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["skeptical", "certain", "grateful", "indifferent"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'The scientists doubted the claim until they could test it themselves, which means they were skeptical.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_002', 'rw', 2, 'Craft and Structure', 'Words in Context', 'Coastal engineers in the Netherlands design movable barriers that can close during storms. These structures cannot stop storms from happening, but they can ____ the damage that rising water causes to nearby cities.', null, 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["ignore", "predict", "mitigate", "worsen"]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'The barriers are meant to reduce flood damage, and mitigate means to make less severe.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_003', 'rw', 2, 'Craft and Structure', 'Words in Context', 'Rather than building a seawall, the town tried a ____ approach that no community in the region had attempted: it planted oyster reefs offshore to break the force of incoming waves.', null, 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["novel", "outdated", "accidental", "familiar"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'The text stresses that no one had tried this approach before, so it was novel, meaning new.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_004', 'rw', 2, 'Craft and Structure', 'Words in Context', 'Naturalist Maria Sibylla Merian was ____ in her study of insects. She recorded every stage of a butterfly''s life cycle and checked each drawing against the living insect several times before she was satisfied.', null, 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["uncertain", "casual", "meticulous", "hasty"]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'Recording every detail and checking each measurement three times is extremely careful, or meticulous.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_005', 'rw', 2, 'Craft and Structure', 'Words in Context', 'After reviewing ten years of traffic data and interviewing hundreds of commuters, the committee concluded that the plan to add bike lanes was financially and practically sound.', null, 'As used in the text, what does the word "sound" most nearly mean?', '["reliable", "loud", "healthy", "deep"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'The text says the plan is based on careful research, so "sound" means reliable or well-founded.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_006', 'rw', 2, 'Craft and Structure', 'Words in Context', 'Most historians believe the poem was written in the 1600s. A few, however, ____ that certain words in it did not exist until the next century, suggesting a later date.', null, 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["conceal", "contend", "deny", "forget"]'::jsonb, 1, null, 'Some historians argue a position that others dispute, which is what contend means.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_007', 'rw', 2, 'Craft and Structure', 'Words in Context', 'Fifty years ago, few households owned a computer. Today, computing devices are ____: they are found in nearly every home, school, and pocket.', null, 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["ubiquitous", "expensive", "fragile", "rare"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'Something found in nearly every home and pocket is everywhere, or ubiquitous.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_008', 'rw', 2, 'Craft and Structure', 'Words in Context', 'The researchers were confident in their results until they learned that some of their water samples had been stored in unclean containers. That discovery threatened to ____ their conclusion that the river was safe to swim in.', null, 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["confirm", "undermine", "illustrate", "strengthen"]'::jsonb, 1, null, 'Evidence that the samples were contaminated would weaken the conclusion, which is what undermine means.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_009', 'rw', 2, 'Craft and Structure', 'Text Structure and Purpose', 'For decades, scientists assumed that octopuses, which live alone, had little reason to communicate with one another. They were thought to avoid each other except to mate. But in 2017, divers off the coast of Australia documented a site where more than a dozen octopuses lived close together, signaling to one another with changes in color and posture.', null, 'Which choice best describes the function of the third sentence in the overall structure of the text?', '["It restates the assumption in different words", "It presents a finding that challenges the assumption described earlier", "It gives an example that supports the assumption", "It describes a method the researchers later abandoned"]'::jsonb, 1, null, 'The first sentences give a long-held belief; the third sentence reports evidence that contradicts it.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_010', 'rw', 2, 'Craft and Structure', 'Text Structure and Purpose', 'In the 1940s, geneticist Barbara McClintock spent years studying the colorful kernels of corn plants, tracking how patterns changed from one generation to the next. Her patient observations revealed that some genes can move to new positions on a chromosome. The idea was so surprising that it took decades to be widely accepted, but in 1983 she received the Nobel Prize for it.', null, 'Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?', '["To criticize scientists who ignored a discovery", "To describe the history of a university", "To explain how a scientist''s unusual method led to an important discovery", "To argue that all scientists should study plants"]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'The text describes McClintock''s patient study of corn and how it led to her Nobel Prize-winning discovery.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_011', 'rw', 2, 'Craft and Structure', 'Text Structure and Purpose', 'Many people believe that humans use only 10 percent of their brains. Brain scans tell a different story. Even during simple tasks such as reading or resting, activity appears across nearly every region of the brain, and damage to almost any area causes noticeable effects.', null, 'Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?', '["It compares two scientists'' theories and favors one", "It presents a problem and lists several solutions", "It describes a historical event in the order it happened", "It describes a common belief and then explains why it is mistaken"]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'The text states the belief that we use only 10% of the brain and then explains evidence that this is false.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_012', 'rw', 2, 'Craft and Structure', 'Text Structure and Purpose', 'The newly discovered frog species lives only on a single mountain in Ecuador. Its forest home is protected, so the species appears safe, at least for now. Researchers plan to monitor the population closely as nearby farms expand.', null, 'Which choice best describes the function of the phrase "at least for now" in the text?', '["It emphasizes how long the problem has existed", "It suggests that the current situation could change in the future", "It shows that the author is certain about the future", "It introduces a quotation from an expert"]'::jsonb, 1, null, '"At least for now" signals that the statement is true at present but may not stay true.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_013', 'rw', 2, 'Craft and Structure', 'Cross-Text Connections', 'Text 1
Teachers often worry that video games shorten students'' attention spans. Fast-moving games, they argue, train players to expect constant excitement, making slower tasks like reading feel dull.

Text 2
In a study by researcher Daphne Bavelier, people who played certain action video games performed better on tests of visual attention than people who did not. The findings suggest that the effects of gaming depend on the type of game.', null, 'Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1 that video games harm students'' focus?', '["By agreeing that all video games should be banned", "By pointing out that certain kinds of games may actually improve attention", "By arguing that focus does not matter for learning", "By claiming that students do not play video games"]'::jsonb, 1, null, 'Text 2 reports research showing some action games improved attention, which challenges Text 1''s general claim.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_014', 'rw', 2, 'Craft and Structure', 'Cross-Text Connections', 'Text 1
Community gardens give city residents access to fresh vegetables and a place to meet their neighbors. In many neighborhoods, they are the only green space within walking distance.

Text 2
Even small city gardens can support surprising numbers of bees, butterflies, and birds. Researchers in London counted more than 30 bee species in a single neighborhood''s gardens.', null, 'Which choice best describes a difference in how the authors of Text 1 and Text 2 view city gardens?', '["Text 1 discusses farms, while Text 2 discusses parks", "Both texts argue that gardens are too expensive to build", "Text 1 focuses on gardens'' benefits to people, while Text 2 focuses on their benefits to wildlife", "Text 1 supports city gardens, while Text 2 opposes them"]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'Text 1 talks about fresh food and community for residents; Text 2 talks about bees and birds.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_015', 'rw', 2, 'Information and Ideas', 'Central Ideas and Details', 'Elephants make deep rumbling calls, some too low for humans to hear. These sounds travel not only through the air but also through the ground. Research by biologist Caitlin O''Connell-Rodwell showed that elephants can sense these vibrations through their feet and trunks, allowing them to communicate with herds several miles away.', null, 'Which choice best states the main idea of the text?', '["Scientists have found that elephants can communicate through vibrations in the ground", "Elephants walk long distances to find water", "Scientists disagree about how elephants sleep", "Elephants have the largest ears of any land animal"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'The whole text explains how elephants send and sense low-frequency vibrations through the ground.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_016', 'rw', 2, 'Information and Ideas', 'Central Ideas and Details', 'Several rural districts switched to a four-day school week to save money on buses and heating. Many parents expected students'' test scores to fall because of the shorter week. A study of the districts found, however, that scores were roughly unchanged, though students in some districts had less time for activities like art and music.', null, 'According to the text, what was surprising about the results of the four-day school week study?', '["Student test scores stayed about the same", "Students spent more time at school", "Bus costs increased sharply", "Teachers asked to return to a five-day week"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'The text says many expected scores to drop, but scores were roughly unchanged.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_017', 'rw', 2, 'Information and Ideas', 'Central Ideas and Details', 'In 1977, Kenyan biologist Wangari Maathai founded the Green Belt Movement, which paid women to plant trees. The trees held soil in place and provided firewood and shade. The women, many of whom had never earned money of their own, gained income and leadership experience. By the time Maathai won the Nobel Peace Prize in 2004, the movement had planted tens of millions of trees.', null, 'Which choice best states the main idea of the text?', '["Wangari Maathai''s tree-planting movement helped both the environment and the women who led it", "Wangari Maathai was the first woman in Kenya to earn a degree", "Kenya''s forests were once larger than they are today", "Planting trees is the best way to stop soil erosion"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'The text describes both environmental results (trees, soil) and benefits for women (income and skills).', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_018', 'rw', 2, 'Information and Ideas', 'Central Ideas and Details', 'Dark roofs absorb most of the sunlight that hits them and can heat up to more than 150°F on summer afternoons. In cities such as New York, volunteers have painted thousands of roofs white. Because white surfaces reflect much of the sunlight, the buildings beneath them stay cooler and need less air conditioning.', null, 'Based on the text, why do some cities paint roofs white?', '["White roofs last longer in heavy rain", "White paint is cheaper than other colors", "White surfaces reflect sunlight, keeping buildings cooler", "Laws require all new roofs to be white"]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'The text says white roofs reflect sunlight instead of absorbing it, lowering indoor temperatures.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_019', 'rw', 2, 'Information and Ideas', 'Command of Evidence', 'A researcher claims that covering garden soil with mulch helps it hold water during hot weather.', '{"h": ["Plot type", "Soil moisture lost over 6 weeks (%)"], "r": [["With mulch", "8"], ["Without mulch", "27"]]}'::jsonb, 'Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the researcher''s claim?', '["The experiment lasted for six weeks", "Plots without mulch lost 27 percent of their soil moisture", "Plots with mulch lost 8 percent of their soil moisture, while plots without mulch lost 27 percent", "Both kinds of plots lost some soil moisture"]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'The claim compares mulched and unmulched plots, so the evidence must give both numbers and show the difference.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_020', 'rw', 2, 'Information and Ideas', 'Command of Evidence', 'In a novel, the character Ellen is described as extremely careful with money.', null, 'Which quotation from the novel most effectively illustrates the claim?', '["\"Ellen loved the smell of the bakery on cold mornings.\"", "\"Ellen counted every coin twice before handing it over, and she wrote each purchase in a little notebook she kept in her apron.\"", "\"Ellen''s brother teased her about her bright red scarf.\"", "\"The market was crowded with shoppers that Saturday.\""]'::jsonb, 1, null, 'Counting coins twice and recording every purchase shows Ellen is very careful with money.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_021', 'rw', 2, 'Information and Ideas', 'Command of Evidence', 'A biologist hypothesizes that some city birds now sing at higher pitches so their songs can be heard over low-pitched traffic noise.', null, 'Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken the hypothesis?', '["Some bird species avoid cities entirely", "Birds in quiet rural areas also sing at higher pitches than they did decades ago", "Traffic noise is louder in cities than in the countryside", "City birds sing at higher pitches than rural birds"]'::jsonb, 1, null, 'If birds in quiet areas changed the same way, city noise could not be the cause.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_022', 'rw', 2, 'Information and Ideas', 'Command of Evidence', 'An energy report shows that in the region studied, wind power produced more electricity than solar power every year from 2000 to 2009. Starting in 2011, solar output passed wind output and kept growing. This shows that ____.', null, 'Which choice most effectively uses data from the graph description to complete the statement?', '["wind power stopped producing electricity in 2010", "only after 2010 did solar power produce more electricity than wind power in the region", "both sources produced the same amount every year", "solar power has always produced more electricity than wind power"]'::jsonb, 1, null, 'The data show wind ahead before 2010 and solar ahead after 2010.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_023', 'rw', 2, 'Information and Ideas', 'Inferences', 'Archaeologists studying an ancient city found scraps of cloth colored with a blue dye. The dye comes from a plant that does not grow within hundreds of miles of the city. The archaeologists concluded that ____', null, 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', '["the dye was invented in the year the cloth was found", "the city''s residents never wore blue clothing", "blue was the least popular color in the city", "the dye was likely made from plants that grew far from the city"]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'No plants that produce the dye grew nearby, so the dye most likely came from plants grown elsewhere.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_024', 'rw', 2, 'Information and Ideas', 'Inferences', 'In a trial of a new headache medicine, 60 percent of patients who took the medicine said their headaches improved. However, 45 percent of patients who unknowingly took sugar pills also reported improvement. This suggests that ____', null, 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', '["patients should never be given sugar pills", "the medicine is harmful to patients", "the medicine itself may not have caused all of the improvement", "the sugar pills worked better than the medicine"]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'Patients who took sugar pills also improved, so some of the improvement may not have come from the medicine.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_025', 'rw', 2, 'Information and Ideas', 'Inferences', 'For twenty years, researchers recorded the date when a flock of ducks left a northern lake each fall. In years when the lake stayed warm into November, the ducks left later than usual. In years when it cooled early, they left earlier. The researchers concluded that ____', null, 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', '["the lake''s temperature affects when the birds begin their migration", "the birds prefer cold water to warm water", "the lake will dry up in the coming years", "the birds will stop migrating altogether"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'The birds left later when the lake stayed warm, which suggests that water temperature influences the timing.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_026', 'rw', 2, 'Information and Ideas', 'Inferences', 'A letter long believed to be by a famous author was recently examined by handwriting experts. The handwriting does not match any of the author''s known letters, but it closely matches documents written by her assistant. This suggests that ____', null, 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', '["the author did not write the letter herself", "the author never received any letters", "the handwriting expert was mistaken", "the letter was written in the 1800s"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'The handwriting doesn''t match the author''s, and it matches her assistant''s, so she probably didn''t write it.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_027', 'rw', 2, 'Standard English Conventions', 'Boundaries', 'The botanist collected seeds from more than two hundred kinds of desert ____ she stored them in a freezer to protect them for future generations.', null, 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["plants, and", "plants. And,", "plants; and", "plants and"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'When "and" joins two complete sentences, a comma goes before it.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_028', 'rw', 2, 'Standard English Conventions', 'Boundaries', 'The Dust Bowl—a series of dust storms that swept across the Great Plains during the ____ forced thousands of families to leave their farms.', null, 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["1930s—", "1930s;", "1930s", "1930s,"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'The dashes set off the added information about the Dust Bowl, so the second dash must match the first.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_029', 'rw', 2, 'Standard English Conventions', 'Boundaries', 'Celia Cruz, a singer ____ the "Queen of Salsa," recorded dozens of albums during her career.', null, 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["known: as", "known, as", "known as,", "known as"]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'No punctuation is needed inside the phrase "known as the ''Queen of Salsa.''"', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_030', 'rw', 2, 'Standard English Conventions', 'Boundaries', 'Deep-sea anglerfish live where sunlight never reaches. They do not rely on outside ____ they produce their own glow using bacteria that live inside a lure on their heads.', null, 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["light, instead,", "light. Instead,", "light, instead", "light instead,"]'::jsonb, 1, null, '"Instead" begins a new complete sentence, so a period comes before it and a comma after it.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_031', 'rw', 2, 'Standard English Conventions', 'Boundaries', 'Many of Earth''s tropical regions have only two ____ a rainy season and a dry season.', null, 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["seasons", "seasons,", "seasons;", "seasons:"]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'A colon introduces an explanation after a complete sentence.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_032', 'rw', 2, 'Standard English Conventions', 'Boundaries', 'Denali, located in south-central ____ tallest mountain in North America, rises more than 20,000 feet above sea level.', null, 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["Alaska; the", "Alaska. The", "Alaska, the", "Alaska the"]'::jsonb, 2, null, '"The tallest mountain in North America" describes Denali and is set off by commas.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_033', 'rw', 2, 'Standard English Conventions', 'Form, Structure, and Sense', 'According to the latest census, the number of residents who commute by bicycle ____ doubled since 2010.', null, 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["are having", "were", "has", "have"]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'The subject is "the number" (singular), so the verb is "has."', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_034', 'rw', 2, 'Standard English Conventions', 'Form, Structure, and Sense', 'Hidden in the attic of the old farmhouse, the letters and diaries of the Delgado family ____ discovered by a plumber in 2015.', null, 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["has been", "were", "was", "is"]'::jsonb, 1, null, 'The subject "the letters and diaries" is plural, so the verb must be "were."', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_035', 'rw', 2, 'Standard English Conventions', 'Form, Structure, and Sense', 'By the time the rescue boat arrived at the island, the stranded hikers ____ on a passing fishing boat.', null, 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["already leaves", "is already leaving", "had already left", "will already leave"]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'The leaving happened before the past event (arriving), so the past perfect "had already left" is correct.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_036', 'rw', 2, 'Standard English Conventions', 'Form, Structure, and Sense', 'The company announced that ____ new electric truck can travel more than 500 miles on a single charge.', null, 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["its", "it''s", "they''re", "their"]'::jsonb, 0, null, 'The possessive "its" refers to the singular "company."', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_037', 'rw', 2, 'Standard English Conventions', 'Form, Structure, and Sense', 'Volunteers in the reef survey spent the day recording fish species, photographing coral, and ____ water temperatures.', null, 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["to measure", "measured", "measures", "measuring"]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'The sentence lists actions in parallel: recording, photographing, and measuring.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_038', 'rw', 2, 'Standard English Conventions', 'Form, Structure, and Sense', 'Mae Jemison trained as both a doctor and an engineer. In 1992, ____ became the first African American woman to travel into space.', null, 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["them", "they", "she", "her"]'::jsonb, 2, null, 'The pronoun is the subject of "won," and it refers to one woman, so "she" is correct.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_039', 'rw', 2, 'Expression of Ideas', 'Transitions', 'The expedition''s route crossed miles of shifting ice, and storms delayed the team for nearly two weeks. ____ the explorers reached the research station before their supplies ran out.', null, 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["For instance,", "Likewise,", "Therefore,", "Nevertheless,"]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'Despite the difficulty described, the team kept going, so a contrast word like "Nevertheless" fits.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_040', 'rw', 2, 'Expression of Ideas', 'Transitions', 'Tardigrades can survive temperatures near absolute zero, pressures six times greater than those in the deepest ocean trench, and years without water. ____ these tiny animals can endure conditions that would kill nearly any other living thing.', null, 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["Even so,", "Meanwhile,", "In contrast,", "In other words,"]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'The second sentence restates the first in simpler terms.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_041', 'rw', 2, 'Expression of Ideas', 'Transitions', 'By the 1920s, wolves had disappeared from Yellowstone National Park. ____ the park''s elk population grew rapidly, and the elk ate so many young willow trees along the rivers that the trees nearly vanished.', null, 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["Similarly,", "Consequently,", "Nonetheless,", "For example,"]'::jsonb, 1, null, 'The loss of the wolves caused the elk to increase, so a cause-and-effect transition fits.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_042', 'rw', 2, 'Expression of Ideas', 'Transitions', 'At 3 a.m., most of the city is asleep and its streets are quiet. ____ in hundreds of small bakeries, workers are already shaping dough for the morning''s bread.', null, 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["In conclusion,", "Therefore,", "For example,", "Meanwhile,"]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'Two things happen at the same time (the city sleeps while bakers work), so "Meanwhile" fits.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_043', 'rw', 2, 'Expression of Ideas', 'Transitions', 'Electric buses produce no exhaust, which improves air quality on busy streets. ____ they are much quieter than diesel buses, reducing noise in neighborhoods along their routes.', null, 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["Instead,", "Moreover,", "However,", "Otherwise,"]'::jsonb, 1, null, 'The second sentence adds another advantage, so "Moreover" fits.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_044', 'rw', 2, 'Expression of Ideas', 'Rhetorical Synthesis', 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• René Laennec invented the stethoscope in France in 1816.
• It lets doctors hear sounds inside the body.
• Wilhelm Röntgen discovered X-rays in 1895 and won a Nobel Prize for it.
• X-ray machines let doctors see bones inside the body.
• Neither invention requires cutting into the body.', null, 'The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the two inventions. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '["René Laennec invented the stethoscope in France.", "The stethoscope was invented in 1816, and the X-ray machine was invented in 1895.", "Wilhelm Röntgen won a Nobel Prize for discovering X-rays.", "Both the stethoscope and the X-ray machine let doctors examine the inside of the body without surgery."]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'Only this choice states something the two inventions have in common.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_045', 'rw', 2, 'Expression of Ideas', 'Rhetorical Synthesis', 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• The Great Barrier Reef is the world''s largest coral reef system.
• It stretches about 1,400 miles off the northeast coast of Australia.
• More than 1,500 species of fish live there.
• It became a World Heritage Site in 1981.
• Rising ocean temperatures have damaged parts of it.', null, 'The student wants to introduce the Great Barrier Reef to an audience unfamiliar with it. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '["The reef has been harmed by rising ocean temperatures.", "The reef was named a World Heritage Site in 1981.", "More than 1,500 species of fish live there.", "Stretching about 1,400 miles off the coast of Australia, the Great Barrier Reef is the world''s largest coral reef system."]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'An introduction should say what and where the reef is; this choice does both.', 'published', 'import', now()),
('rwl2_046', 'rw', 2, 'Expression of Ideas', 'Rhetorical Synthesis', 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:
• Researchers studied how college students take notes during lectures.
• The study took place at two universities.
• Some students wrote notes by hand; others typed on laptops.
• A week later, students who wrote by hand remembered more of the lecture.', null, 'The student wants to explain the result of the study. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '["Some students in the study typed their notes on laptops.", "The study took place at two universities.", "Researchers studied how college students take notes.", "Students who took handwritten notes remembered more of the lecture a week later than students who typed their notes."]'::jsonb, 3, null, 'Only this choice states what the study found.', 'published', 'import', now())
on conflict (id) do nothing;
